WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.834
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:03.369
is a Golden Globe
award-winning actor

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:05.337
you know, from movies
such as "Dune,"

00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:07.840
"Inside Llewyn Davis,"
and the "Star Wars" franchise.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.977
He stars in "Moon Knight," which
is streaming now on Disney+,

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.812
with new episodes
released every Wednesday.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.013
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:16.182
-I wish I could just disappear.
I really do.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.685
But, unfortunately,
I'm still here.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.019
And if you are gonna
go through with this,

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:22.288
you've got to be smart,
at least for Layla's sake.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.190
I've been in situations
like this before.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.192
-So have I.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:28.294
It's the same body, isn't it?

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.995
It's in there somewhere.

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:31.431
Muscle memory in that?

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:32.732
-Yeah. I'm not sure
it works that way.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:34.100
Just...
-Oh, whatever.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.503
-I'm here. You're not alone.
-I know I'm not alone.

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:40.473
I know I'm bloody not alone.
I've got Layla.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:43.442
She's got my back.
-Oh, are you in love?

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:45.745
Now you're in love with my wife?
-I appreciate your concern,

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:47.280
mate, I really do.
-You lay one finger on her...

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.482
-But we've got it from here.
-...I swear to you, Stephen --

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:50.950
-That's what I need,
not a recipe

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.219
for a protein shake.
I'll call you.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.420
-I'll throw us off a cliff!

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.521
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:55.521 --> 00:00:57.724
our friend Oscar Isaac,
everybody.

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.758
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.758 --> 00:01:02.762
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:10.736
♪♪

00:01:10.736 --> 00:01:16.943
♪♪

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:20.013
Ah, it is so nice to see you.
-Thank you, buddy.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.981
Thank you.
-I mean, from that clip we saw,

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.950
you got to live
a lot of actors' dreams.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.485
You got to do a scene
with Oscar Isaac.

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:28.121
-I mean, it was the best.
-It was the best.

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:29.788
He's as delightful
as everyone says.

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:31.890
-So charismatic.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.459
Yeah.
-So, you do get to play

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:35.361
two versions of yourself
in this show,

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:37.230
because you are playing
a character

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.833
who has dissociative identity
disorder.

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.968
-At least two.
-At least two, right.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.403
-At the moment.
-I'm so careful,

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.337
when it's a Marvel property,

00:01:45.337 --> 00:01:48.574
to have to stop
at the minimum of information.

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:49.942
-Mm-hmm.
-You know what I mean?

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:51.544
I would never -- 'cause
I just don't want to risk it.

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.246
-You don't want to be that guy.
-I don't want to be that guy.

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.748
-[ Chuckling ] No.
-But, you know, we heard there

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.218
you get to have an American
accent and a British accent.

00:01:58.218 --> 00:02:00.252
Was the British accent something
that was there

00:02:00.252 --> 00:02:01.854
from the beginning,
or something you brought to it?

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:04.290
-Yeah. No, it was,
when I got --

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.092
when Kevin contacted me,

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:09.129
he basically just sent
the first and the fifth episode,

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:11.231
and they were
early drafts of them.

00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:13.966
And I saw that it was
set in London,

00:02:13.966 --> 00:02:16.469
but the character
wasn't written as English.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.204
And I asked
why it was set in London.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.873
And basically the reason was
because they have

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:23.009
so many stories in New York.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:25.244
-And they didn't want Marvel
to just be New York.

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:27.213
The world is a little bigger
than just New York.

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:30.183
And so -- And so they
set it in London.

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.417
They just made him an expat.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:35.320
But I saw an opportunity
to do something really fun

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.990
and kind of pay an homage
to all the stuff that I love.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.992
Like, I was obsessed
with "The Office," you know?

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:42.995
I had, like, all the DVDs,
back when DVDs existed.

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:45.631
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.
-And you know, so it just --

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:47.299
-It's a very bold choice
'cause, of course,

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:48.467
you were doing a British accent

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:50.469
and you're surrounded
by British actors.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:52.338
-And the crew.
The crew is --

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.540
it was a large majority
of people from Budapest,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.976
where we shot.
And you know,

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:58.611
London, like,
working-class guys,

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.512
which was great because I could
always go to them and be like,

00:03:00.512 --> 00:03:01.714
"How would you say this?
What would you say?

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.449
How do I sound?
Does that sound okay?

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.317
They're like, "Yeah, yeah, mate,
it's alright."

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.720
[ Laughter ]
So, no, but it was --

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.788
you know, it's really
a testament to Kevin

00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:13.526
that, you know, I kind of
came up with this voice

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.128
and I started finding it
and I would do it for my kids,

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:19.098
and they'd laugh and keep asking
me to do it over and over again.

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:20.433
I'm like, "Alright,
I'm onto something here."

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:23.069
And then I was like,
"Alright, Kevin, if I do it,

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.670
I'd like to do it like this."

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.873
And they're like, "Okay."
[ Laughter ] You know?

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:29.542
-That's the reaction
every actor wants.

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:31.577
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:31.577 --> 00:03:33.512
-And so they went for it,
you know.

00:03:33.512 --> 00:03:35.214
And afterwards, he's like, "We
weren't sure it was gonna work,

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:36.449
but we really wanted you
to do this thing."

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:38.184
And luckily, it works.
-That's great.

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:39.719
That's interesting
that your kids, like --

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.287
So, when I read books
to my kids --

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:43.022
and our boys are
about the same age --

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:46.692
they hate when I do voices
and they hate when I do accents.

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:49.762
Now, that just might be the gap
in our skills as actors.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:51.196
-It might be that.
-Yeah,

00:03:51.196 --> 00:03:54.267
-I went to Juilliard.
-You went to Juilliard, yeah.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.634
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.136
You know what my kids hate?
When I sing.

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:58.537
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:03:58.537 --> 00:03:59.806
You have a beautiful
singing voice.

00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:02.442
-I know.
I tell them all the time.

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:04.276
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:05.978
It was my kid's birthday
yesterday.

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:09.482
He turned 5, and we got him
a karaoke microphone.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:11.684
And I've just been using it
nonstop.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:13.853
[ Laughter ]
And even this morning,

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.321
like, the little one,
the 2 1/2-year-old,

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:17.389
he's like, "Dad, stop, stop."

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:19.592
He started crying and crying
until I stopped.

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:23.295
-Do you think they're just --
that's the first moment

00:04:23.295 --> 00:04:25.531
they've realized their dad
is needy for attention?

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.200
-Not the first time.
-Not the first time.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.202
-No, they've got
a really needy dad.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.904
-But, now,
we were talking backstage.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.540
The last time I saw you was
the Thursday before you did

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.608
an excellent job hosting "SNL."
-Thank you, man.

00:04:36.608 --> 00:04:38.277
-But there is something --
-You walked in

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:39.845
and wished me luck
when I was over there.

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:41.947
-I love to do it on Thursday
with friends hosting.

00:04:41.947 --> 00:04:45.551
But you had -- you did have
a sketch that broke through

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.419
with the demo of 2 1/2 to 5.

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:49.488
-Yeah, and it's not
the one you would think,

00:04:49.488 --> 00:04:51.024
'cause you'd think
it's the PAW Patrol.

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:52.891
-You did a PAW Patrol sketch.
-I did a PAW Patrol sketch.

00:04:52.891 --> 00:04:55.361
My kids love PAW Patrol.
They weren't as into it.

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:56.796
-Yeah.
-They were like,

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:58.631
"Don't mess with
our sacred show."

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:02.735
-But they really Meatball Man.

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.703
-They loved...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.703 --> 00:05:06.639
That's the only version
of me singing they'll take.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.307
-Yes.
-As Meatball Man.

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:10.276
-There's Sarah, there, and
you are meatball on her neck.

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:12.612
This is a sketch really worth
watching if anybody missed it.

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.948
And you know, I realized --
I was watching it today.

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.483
Once I heard your kids
really liked it, I'm like,

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.084
"Oh, I bet my kids
would like this, too."

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:21.987
-Yeah, yeah.
It freaked out my cousin's kids.

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:24.390
They got really weirded out
by it.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:28.026
But my kids -- my 5-year-old
just sang the whole song

00:05:28.026 --> 00:05:29.761
this morning on the couch.
-Really?

00:05:29.761 --> 00:05:31.096
-He already knows
"Meatball Man"?

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:32.231
-He knows the whole...

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:33.832
♪ I am little Meatball Man ♪

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.868
♪ Singing my little
meatball song ♪

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:38.103
-How can he say you don't have
a beautiful voice.

00:05:38.103 --> 00:05:40.372
-Right? I don't know.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.372 --> 00:05:41.874
-Hey, this is, uh --

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:44.009
We're huge fans
of Ethan Hawke here.

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:45.744
And one of the cool things
about that --

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:48.981
'cause, you know, "Moon Knight"
is is a lesser-known property.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:50.449
-But you knew about it.
-I knew about it.

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:54.086
But it's really cool because
it it feels like, you know,

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:56.122
not that there's anything indie
in the world of Marvel,

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:59.458
but it feels a little bit more
like indie and that it's you.

00:05:59.458 --> 00:06:00.792
And then also I heard --

00:06:00.792 --> 00:06:03.028
this sounds like the most indie
way to find a costar

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:04.197
for a Marvel property --

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:06.665
that you invited Ethan Hawke
to a coffee shop,

00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:08.734
or maybe just saw him
at a coffee shop, and asked him.

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:11.336
-Ran into him in Brooklyn,
in our funny hats.

00:06:11.336 --> 00:06:13.105
-Yeah, you guys were wearing --

00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:15.641
He had, like, a what?
Like a fox catcher or something?

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:17.210
-Yeah. I just had
a big mustache.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.778
-Yeah.
-No, kind of.

00:06:19.778 --> 00:06:22.414
Actually, that's what happened.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:25.518
Everything about the way
this came together,

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:27.386
it just -- it ended up being,

00:06:27.386 --> 00:06:29.888
like, the version of it
that I hoped it would be,

00:06:29.888 --> 00:06:32.291
but was sure
that it wasn't going to be.

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:33.726
You know, I was sure
that I was, like --

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:35.661
I had all this good intention
I was just gonna end up

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:37.597
on a hill
somewhere in Eastern Europe

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.999
in, like, a cape, being like,
"What the hell am I doing here?"

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:42.134
[ Laughter ]
But, you know,

00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:44.670
just, like, talking to Kevin
and then Mohamed Diab,

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.271
the amazing director, who's --

00:06:46.271 --> 00:06:49.208
if you haven't seen his movies
"Clash" and "Amira,"

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:50.709
he's just
an incredible filmmaker.

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:52.044
And he's the one
that was going to, you know,

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:53.446
be the steward of this thing.

00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:54.847
And we started talking about it.

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:56.482
And I was watching
"The Good Lord Bird"

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:58.751
at the time, and Ethan's
just incredible in it.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:01.220
And I said, "You know,
this guy would be amazing

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.157
for this cult leader,
bad guy that we're developing."

00:07:04.157 --> 00:07:06.892
And he said that he had been
actually working on something

00:07:06.892 --> 00:07:09.795
with Ethan
and that we should do that.

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:11.530
And then, that day,
I went to a coffee shop,

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.467
and there was Ethan with
his daughter, getting coffee.

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:16.869
And I approached him,
and we talked for a little bit.

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:20.339
He didn't know who I was at all.
His daughter had to tell him.

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:23.208
And then -- but we exchanged --
-This is before Meatball Man.

00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:26.111
Before you're a star.
-This is before Meatball Man.

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:27.446
took over the globe.
-Oh, yeah.

00:07:27.446 --> 00:07:30.382
-And then we were like,
"Well, let's get together

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:32.151
and have a drink or something."

00:07:32.151 --> 00:07:34.253
So a couple days later,
we went to Brooklyn Inn

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:37.289
and had many tequilas,
and I convinced him

00:07:37.289 --> 00:07:38.857
to be the bad guy
in "Moon Knight."

00:07:38.857 --> 00:07:41.360
-Very well done.
It paid off great.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.296
-I mean, just that opening scene
in the first episode,

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:45.998
that's all him.
Like, he came up with that.

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:48.868
That's why he --
-It's a joy to watch.

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:50.336
I have a lot more questions
for you.

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:52.905
We'll be right back
with more from Oscar Isaac.

